        "conclusion": "Patient has an elevated Prostate Health Index (phi = 47.8) in this synthetic test result, indicating an elevated probability of clinically significant prostate cancer (Gleason Score >= 7) on biopsy.\n\nClinical Interpretation: The Prostate Health Index is calculated via the formula: phi = ([-2]proPSA / Free PSA) * sqrt(Total PSA). A phi value >= 36.0 is associated with a 36.0% to 55.0% probability of cancer on biopsy. In men aged 50 and older with total PSA in the 4.0 to 10.0 ng/mL diagnostic gray zone, this elevated score provides strong clinical justification for mpMRI and targeted prostate biopsy.\n\nMethodology: Total PSA, Free PSA, and [-2]proPSA are quantitatively measured in serum using Beckman Coulter Access chemiluminescent immunoassays calibrated against WHO standards. The phi score is derived mathematically.\n\nLimitations: phi results should be evaluated in conjunction with digital rectal examination (DRE), prostate volume, family history, and clinical risk factors. SYNTHETIC LABORATORY REPORT - FOR TESTING PURPOSES ONLY - ZERO REAL PATIENT DATA / NO PHI Page 1 of 1"
      },
